","_margin":{"unit":"px","top":"30","right":"0","bottom":"0","left":"0","isLinked":false}},"elements":[],"widgetType":"heading"},{"id":"ef6359d","elType":"widget","settings":{"editor":"<h3><strong>How is AR different from virtual reality (VR)?</strong></h3><p>VR creates a completely immersive digital environment, while AR enhances the real world with digital additions. Think VR headsets vs. using an app on your phone.</p><h3><strong>Do I need special equipment for AR in the classroom?</strong></h3><p>Many AR apps work on smartphones or tablets, which most students already have! Some specialized experiences might use AR glasses, but those are less common in classrooms right now.</p><h3><strong>Is AR just a gimmick, or does it help students learn?</strong></h3><p>Research shows AR boosts engagement, helps with understanding complex topics, and can even improve test scores! It's about making learning more interactive and hands-on.</p><h3><strong>Are there any good examples of AR being used in schools now?</strong></h3><p>Absolutely! Museums offer AR tours, medical schools use AR for anatomy lessons, and even history classes use AR to bring historical events to life.</p>"},"elements":[],"widgetType":"text-editor"}],"isInner":false},{"id":"4b26fb70","elType":"column","settings":{"_column_size":33,"_inline_size":23.171,"hide_tablet":"hidden-tablet","hide_mobile":"hidden-mobile"},"elements":[{"id":"34db76ce","elType":"section","settings":{"height_inner":"min-height","sticky":"top","sticky_offset":100,"sticky_effects_offset":100,"sticky_parent":"yes","the7_sticky_row_overlap":"yes","the7_hide_on_sticky_effect":"show"},"elements":[{"id":"2a2f21ab","elType":"column","settings":{"_column_size":100,"_inline_size":null,"css_classes":"stickForm","hide_tablet":"hidden-tablet","custom_css":".stickForm{
